      "message": "fix(sim): the hole-wall excuse measures a facet about its hole's axis, so a tool cutting into a flat is a gouge\n\nThe containment kinds' (drill, bore, thread mill, cross drill) solid-law\nexcuse read a facet by its corners against the tool's own disc: any facet\nwhose vertices stood outside the tool was forgiven its edges' dip into\nit, a bound that grows as the tool shrinks against the hole. A flat whose\ncorners lie on the hole circle - an octagon, a D-bore, a square - passes\ninside it along its middle, and a cut there went unconvicted (a thread\nmill 0.32 mm into an octagon's flats, a bore 0.38 mm: 0 warnings).\n\nThe operation now names its holes' axes (verify::solid_hits_walled,\nHoleAxis), and a facet is excused only when it is a round wall of one of\nthem: its sagitta about that axis - nearest vertex radius less its\nnearest approach (point-to-segment / point-in-triangle, square to the\naxis) - is at most WALL_SAG (0.05 mm). The excuse is the smaller of that\nsagitta and the old edge-dip bound, so the law never convicts less than\nbefore. Non-finite facets, facets over the axis, axes not along the\nmove's tool axis, and laws with no holes named excuse nothing.\n\nFixes #1205\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
